SEC. 100001. APPLICABILITY OF THE IMMIGRATION LAWS.

This section establishes that the fees in this subtitle apply to aliens in the circumstances described, and that terms used have the meanings given in section 101 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (8 U.S.C. 1101). It also clarifies that any reference to a section or provision in this subtitle refers to the Immigration and Nationality Act, unless otherwise expressly provided.

SEC. 100002. ASYLUM FEE.

This section requires the Secretary of Homeland Security or the Attorney General to collect a fee from any alien filing an asylum application. The initial fee for fiscal year 2025 is the greater of $100 or an amount set by rule, with annual inflation adjustments based on the Consumer Price Index. Fifty percent of fees from applications filed with the Attorney General are credited to the Executive Office for Immigration Review, and 50% of fees from applications filed with the Secretary are credited to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, with any remaining amounts going to the general fund of the Treasury. These fees cannot be waived or reduced.

SEC. 100003. EMPLOYMENT AUTHORIZATION DOCUMENT FEES.

This section imposes a fee on initial employment authorization applications for asylum applicants, parolees, and those with Temporary Protected Status (TPS). The initial fee for each category is the greater of $550 or an amount set by rule, with annual inflation adjustments. For asylum applicants, 25% of fees are credited to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services (with at least 50% of that used for fraud detection), while all fees from parolees and TPS holders go to the general fund of the Treasury. These fees cannot be waived or reduced.

SEC. 100004. IMMIGRATION PAROLE FEE.

This section requires a fee from any alien paroled into the United States, with the initial amount for fiscal year 2025 being the greater of $1,000 or an amount set by rule, subject to annual inflation adjustments. Exceptions are provided for specific circumstances, including medical emergencies, organ donation, attending a funeral of a close family member, and cases where a significant public benefit results from law enforcement assistance. All fees collected are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury. Fees cannot be waived or reduced except for the listed exceptions.

SEC. 100005. SPECIAL IMMIGRANT JUVENILE FEE.

This section requires a fee from any alien, parent, or legal guardian applying for special immigrant juvenile status. The initial fee for fiscal year 2025 is the greater of $250 or an amount set by rule, with annual inflation adjustments. All fees collected are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

SEC. 100006. TEMPORARY PROTECTED STATUS FEE.

This section amends section 244(c)(1)(B) of the Immigration and Nationality Act to increase the maximum fee for Temporary Protected Status from $50 to $500, subject to annual inflation adjustments. All fees collected are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury, and the fees cannot be waived or reduced.

SEC. 100007. VISA INTEGRITY FEE.

This section requires a fee from any alien issued a nonimmigrant visa, with the initial amount for fiscal year 2025 being the greater of $250 or an amount set by rule, subject to annual inflation adjustments. All fees collected are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury, unless reimbursed to the alien. The Secretary of Homeland Security may reimburse the fee if the alien complied with all visa conditions and departed the U.S. within 5 days of the visa's expiration or was granted an extension or adjustment to lawful permanent resident status. Fees cannot be waived or reduced.

SEC. 100008. FORM I-94 FEE.

This section requires a fee from any alien submitting an application for a Form I-94 Arrival/Departure Record. The initial fee for fiscal year 2025 is the greater of $24 or an amount set by rule, with annual inflation adjustments. Twenty percent of the fees collected are deposited into the Land Border Inspection Fee Account for U.S. Customs and Border Protection, and the remaining 80% goes to the general fund of the Treasury. Fees cannot be waived or reduced.

SEC. 100009. ANNUAL ASYLUM FEE.

This section requires an annual fee from any alien whose asylum application remains pending, with the initial amount for fiscal year 2025 being the greater of $100 or an amount set by rule, subject to annual inflation adjustments. All fees collected are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury. Fees cannot be waived or reduced.

SEC. 100010. FEE RELATING TO RENEWAL AND EXTENSION OF EMPLOYMENT AUTHORIZATION FOR PAROLEES.

This section requires a fee for any parolee seeking a renewal or extension of employment authorization, with the initial amount for fiscal year 2025 being the greater of $275 or an amount set by rule, subject to annual inflation adjustments. The employment authorization is valid for 1 year or the duration of the parole, whichever is shorter. Twenty-five percent of the fees are credited to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, and the remaining 75% goes to the general fund of the Treasury. Fees cannot be waived or reduced.

SEC. 100011. FEE RELATING TO RENEWAL OR EXTENSION OF EMPLOYMENT AUTHORIZATION FOR ASYLUM APPLICANTS.

This section requires a fee of not less than $275 for each renewal or extension of employment authorization for asylum applicants. The employment authorization terminates immediately upon denial of the asylum application by an asylum officer (unless referred to an immigration judge), 30 days after an immigration judge's denial (unless appealed), or immediately upon denial by the Board of Immigration Appeals. Twenty-five percent of the fees are credited to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, and the remaining 75% goes to the general fund of the Treasury. Fees cannot be waived or reduced.

SEC. 100012. FEE RELATING TO RENEWAL AND EXTENSION OF EMPLOYMENT AUTHORIZATION FOR ALIENS GRANTED TEMPORARY PROTECTED STATUS.

This section requires a fee for any alien seeking a renewal or extension of employment authorization based on Temporary Protected Status, with the initial amount for fiscal year 2025 being the greater of $275 or an amount set by rule, subject to annual inflation adjustments. The employment authorization is valid for 1 year or the duration of the TPS designation, whichever is shorter. Twenty-five percent of the fees are credited to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, and the remaining 75% goes to the general fund of the Treasury. Fees cannot be waived or reduced.

SEC. 100013. FEES RELATING TO APPLICATIONS FOR ADJUSTMENT OF STATUS.

This section establishes fees for various immigration court applications and appeals, including $1,500 for adjustment of status, $1,050 for a waiver of inadmissibility, $500 for TPS, $900 for appeals of immigration judge or DHS officer decisions, $1,325 for appeals in practitioner disciplinary cases, $900 for motions to reopen/reconsider, $600 for suspension of deportation, $600 for cancellation of removal for permanent residents, and $1,500 for cancellation of removal for nonpermanent residents. All fees are subject to annual inflation adjustments. Up to 25% of the fees are credited to the Executive Office for Immigration Review, with the remainder going to the general fund of the Treasury. The fees cannot be used for the Legal Orientation Program.

SEC. 100014. ELECTRONIC SYSTEM FOR TRAVEL AUTHORIZATION FEE.

This section amends the Electronic System for Travel Authorization (ESTA) fee structure, requiring not less than $13 per travel authorization in addition to the existing fee of not less than $10. The authorization for the fee is extended from October 31, 2028, to October 31, 2034. The fee is subject to annual inflation adjustments based on the Consumer Price Index.

SEC. 100015. ELECTRONIC VISA UPDATE SYSTEM FEE.

This section requires a fee from any alien subject to the Electronic Visa Update System at the time of enrollment, with the initial amount for fiscal year 2025 being the greater of $30 or an amount set by rule, subject to annual inflation adjustments. A new "CBP Electronic Visa Update System Account" is established in the general fund of the Treasury, receiving all fees minus $5 per payment, which is deposited into the general fund. The account funds are appropriated for U.S. Customs and Border Protection costs associated with administering the system. Fees cannot be waived or reduced.

SEC. 100016. FEE FOR ALIENS ORDERED REMOVED IN ABSENTIA.

This section requires a fee from any alien ordered removed in absentia and subsequently arrested by U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, with the initial amount for fiscal year 2025 being the greater of $5,000 or an amount set by rule, subject to annual inflation adjustments. The fee does not apply if the removal order was rescinded. Fifty percent of the fees are credited to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, and the remaining 50% goes to the general fund of the Treasury. Fees cannot be waived or reduced.

SEC. 100017. INADMISSIBLE ALIEN APPREHENSION FEE.

This section requires a fee from any inadmissible alien apprehended between ports of entry, with the initial amount for fiscal year 2025 being the greater of $5,000 or an amount set by rule, subject to annual inflation adjustments. Fifty percent of the fees are credited to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, and the remaining 50% goes to the general fund of the Treasury. All fees collected are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

SEC. 100018. AMENDMENT TO AUTHORITY TO APPLY FOR ASYLUM.

This section amends section 208(d)(3) of the Immigration and Nationality Act to change the Attorney General's authority to set asylum application fees from "may" to "shall." It also removes the previous cap on such fees and clarifies that the Attorney General can set additional adjudication and naturalization fees in accordance with section 286(m).

SEC. 100051. APPROPRIATION FOR THE DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY.

This section appropriates $2,055,000,000 to the Secretary of Homeland Security for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. The funds are for immigration enforcement activities, including hiring and training CBP agents, transportation and removal costs, personnel assignments, background checks, protecting alien children from exploitation, transporting aliens from contiguous territory, state and local participation, removal of specified unaccompanied alien children, expedited removal of criminal aliens, criminal and gang checks for unaccompanied alien children, and information technology investments.

SEC. 100052. APPROPRIATION FOR U.S. IMMIGRATION AND CUSTOMS ENFORCEMENT.

This section appropriates $29,850,000,000 to the Secretary of Homeland Security for U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. The funds are for hiring and training personnel, performance/retention/signing bonuses, recruitment, transportation, information technology, facility upgrades, fleet modernization, family unity, 287(g) agreements, the Victims of Immigration Crime Engagement Office, and the Office of the Principal Legal Advisor.

SEC. 100053. APPROPRIATION FOR FEDERAL LAW ENFORCEMENT TRAINING CENTERS.

This section appropriates $750,000,000 to the Secretary of Homeland Security for the Federal Law Enforcement Training Centers for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. Not less than $285,000,000 is for training newly hired federal law enforcement personnel and state/local law enforcement agencies, and not more than $465,000,000 is for facility procurement, construction, maintenance, and improvements.

SEC. 100054. APPROPRIATION FOR THE DEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE.

This section appropriates $3,330,000,000 to the Attorney General for the Department of Justice for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. The funds are for hiring immigration judges (with a cap of 800 by November 1, 2028), combating drug trafficking, prosecuting immigration matters, nonparty injunctive relief, the Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Grant Program and Office of Community Oriented Policing, fiscally responsible lawsuit settlements, and compensation for incarceration of criminal aliens.

SEC. 100055. BRIDGING IMMIGRATION-RELATED DEFICITS EXPERIENCED NATIONWIDE REIMBURSEMENT FUND.

This section establishes the "Bridging Immigration-related Deficits Experienced Nationwide (BIDEN) Reimbursement Fund" within the Department of Justice. The Attorney General is appropriated up to $3,500,000,000 for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2028, for grants to eligible states and local governments for purposes including locating and apprehending criminal aliens, investigating and prosecuting crimes committed by aliens, and temporary criminal detention. Grants can be for activities that occurred on or after January 20, 2021, and must be distributed to more than one state.

SEC. 100056. APPROPRIATION FOR THE BUREAU OF PRISONS.

This section appropriates $5,000,000,000 to the Director of the Bureau of Prisons for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. Not less than $3,000,000,000 is for hiring and training new employees and salaries/benefits for the current workforce, and not more than $2,000,000,000 is for facility maintenance and repairs.

SEC. 100057. APPROPRIATION FOR THE UNITED STATES SECRET SERVICE.

This section appropriates $1,170,000,000 to the Director of the United States Secret Service for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. The funds are for additional resources, including personnel, training facilities, programming, and technology, and for performance, retention, and signing bonuses for qualified personnel.

SEC. 100101. APPROPRIATION TO THE ADMINISTRATIVE OFFICE OF THE UNITED STATES COURTS.

This section appropriates $1,250,000 for each of fiscal years 2025 through 2028 to the Director of the Administrative Office of the United States Courts. The funds are for continuing analyses and reporting on the state of court dockets, including an assessment of judicial orders issuing non-party relief against the Federal Government and their aggregate cost impact on taxpayers.

SEC. 100102. APPROPRIATION TO THE FEDERAL JUDICIAL CENTER.

This section appropriates $1,000,000 for each of fiscal years 2025 through 2028 to the Director of the Federal Judicial Center. The funds are for continuing education and training programs for judicial branch personnel, including training on the absence of constitutional and statutory authority supporting legal claims that seek non-party relief against the Federal Government.

SEC. 100201. EXTENSION OF FUND.

This section amends the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act to extend the termination date of the fund from its previous date to December 31, 2028.

SEC. 100202. CLAIMS RELATING TO ATMOSPHERIC TESTING.

This section expands eligibility for compensation under the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act for claims related to atmospheric testing. It extends the relevant time period for physical presence in affected areas to November 6, 1962, adds a new category for individuals physically present for at least 1 year between September 24, 1944, and November 6, 1962, and increases compensation amounts for leukemia and specified diseases to $100,000. It also expands the definition of "affected area" to include the states of New Mexico, Utah, and Idaho, and specific counties in Nevada and Arizona.

SEC. 100203. CLAIMS RELATING TO URANIUM MINING.

This section expands eligibility for compensation under the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act for claims related to uranium mining. It expands the list of states where employment in uranium mines or mills qualifies, adds "core driller" as a qualifying position, and adds renal cancer and other chronic renal diseases to the list of compensable diseases. It also establishes special rules for individuals with combined work histories in multiple qualifying positions.

SEC. 100204. CLAIMS RELATING TO MANHATTAN PROJECT WASTE.

This section adds a new section 5A to the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act to provide compensation for claims related to Manhattan Project waste. It provides $50,000 (or more for documented medical expenses) to living affected individuals and $25,000 to surviving spouses or children of deceased individuals. Eligibility requires physical presence for at least 2 years after January 1, 1949, in specified ZIP codes in Missouri, Tennessee, Alaska, and Kentucky, and contraction of a specified disease.

SEC. 100205. LIMITATIONS ON CLAIMS.

This section amends the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act to change the deadline for filing claims from "2 years after the date of enactment of the RECA Extension Act of 2022" to December 31, 2027. [end of text]


